  "id": "Compound::DB00136 - Disease::DOID:11476",
  "verbalization": "Calcitriol is implicated in the pathogenesis of osteoporosis through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Calcitriol may contribute to the development of osteoporosis by inducing calcinosis, which is associated with calcium acetate, a dietary supplement that is known to treat osteoporosis. Similarly, Calcitriol is linked to nephrocalcinosis, caused by conjugated estrogens, which also has therapeutic effects on osteoporosis. These pathways highlight a potential role of Calcitriol in the regulation of calcium metabolism and bone density, though the exact mechanism by which Calcitriol influences osteoporosis remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence from these paths indicates that Calcitriol may act as a mediator in the treatment of osteoporosis through its effects on calcium-related disorders, but further research is needed to establish the precise causal relationship and the role of Calcitriol in the broader context of osteoporosis pathophysiology.",
